$\n{z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $4$ times the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{4{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298679 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298680 13194139534610 true] [17592186298679 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298681 13194139534610 true] [17592186298679 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298682 13194139534610 true] [17592186298679 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298683 13194139534610 true] [17592186298679 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298684 13194139534610 true] [17592186298679 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534610 true] [17592186298679 1041 "selection" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298679 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298680 13194139534610 true] [17592186298679 1110 #uuid "7791552b-c126-59c8-a32e-8b164e58dee3" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298680 1034 "A cone" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298680 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298680 1111 #uuid "3ac8f046-72df-576f-b21c-21006508677d" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298681 1034 "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298681 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298681 1111 #uuid "0e5b890b-c961-51ca-b0e7-db193ae6f885" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298682 1034 "A sphere" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298682 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298682 1111 #uuid "29bdfdcc-e59d-5c17-b89d-3d7243da78b4" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298683 1034 "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298683 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298683 1111 #uuid "5994d573-9f3d-548c-945d-3db9786a649e" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298684 1034 "A circular cylinder" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298684 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298684 1111 #uuid "efe2136f-2d64-5094-b77a-5e5d844a1c3b" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298685 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298686 13194139534610 true] [17592186298685 1089 "q-104241" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298685 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534610 true] [17592186298685 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=\\frac{1}{5}r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298685 1113 #uuid "0d1330ed-ad27-5e66-8808-a6a63e5c430b" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298685 1145 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{1}{5}r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals one-fifth the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{(\\frac{1}{5}r)}=5.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298687 13194139534610 true] [17592186298686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298688 13194139534610 true] [17592186298686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298689 13194139534610 true] [17592186298686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298690 13194139534610 true] [17592186298686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298691 13194139534610 true] [17592186298686 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534610 true] [17592186298686 1041 "selection" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298686 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298690 13194139534610 true] [17592186298686 1110 #uuid "ebb1c09e-c17a-5dbc-98ad-a9fa87e9637d" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298687 1034 "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298687 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298687 1111 #uuid "71344778-774e-5983-9411-00f1674cccad" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298688 1034 "A sphere" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298688 1111 #uuid "03eaa07a-3e3b-58a1-a6b4-eb99bb210eea" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298689 1034 "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298689 1111 #uuid "edf9dcb0-d9fc-58a1-a425-749613cb6369" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298690 1034 "A half-cone" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298690 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298690 1111 #uuid "f4cab9ef-7313-5f90-97fc-aae7b83957b5" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298691 1034 "A circular cylinder" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298691 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534610 true] [17592186298691 1111 #uuid "38433e0a-17e0-5b5e-a3b6-f42677e1ee7e" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298692 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298693 13194139534610 true] [17592186298692 1089 "q-104243" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298692 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534610 true] [17592186298692 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/446989d58605132a7e8e361b12127341c490845d893a792662b41c3d086e71e6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298692 1113 #uuid "aaf763ca-bf8e-52b8-944d-3a49a19773cf" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298692 1145 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular half-cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=r.$" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298693 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298694 13194139534610 true] [17592186298693 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298695 13194139534610 true] [17592186298693 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298696 13194139534610 true] [17592186298693 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298697 13194139534610 true] [17592186298693 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298698 13194139534610 true] [17592186298693 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534610 true] [17592186298693 1041 "selection" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298693 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298697 13194139534610 true] [17592186298693 1110 #uuid "820d1dd5-a82c-5083-8f31-61009dce45b9" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298694 1034 "z=\\pi r" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298694 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298694 1111 #uuid "76093b81-ef74-5a2f-9f49-6725ef31434b" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298695 1034 "z=\\frac{r}{\\pi }" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298695 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298695 1111 #uuid "dcb27d5a-d9c2-5167-bfaa-321f4e3de7a7" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298696 1034 "{z}^{2}=\\frac{{r}^{2}}{\\pi }" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298696 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298696 1111 #uuid "7aa91e70-d925-56c0-96e4-a832d5c83bdb" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298697 1034 "z=r" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298697 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298697 1111 #uuid "ab77c22f-f720-54d6-b3e3-3a2764fe22dd" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298698 1034 "{z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298698 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298698 1111 #uuid "20bd8848-9e0c-5d09-96f1-ac15185f1393" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298699 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298700 13194139534610 true] [17592186298699 1089 "q-104329" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298699 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534610 true] [17592186298699 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/a89469b49ecb4f40ba8378e1ce5404f5a1cf0eb4e78922529d9dce368b28c82d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298699 1113 #uuid "d8134d34-0cf4-5c60-943e-99085b23f341" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298699 1145 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =1 \\\\ z & =r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}={r}^{2}.$" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298700 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298701 13194139534610 true] [17592186298700 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298702 13194139534610 true] [17592186298700 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298703 13194139534610 true] [17592186298700 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298704 13194139534610 true] [17592186298700 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298705 13194139534610 true] [17592186298700 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534610 true] [17592186298700 1041 "selection" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298700 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298702 13194139534610 true] [17592186298700 1110 #uuid "f388930e-6b64-5ca7-b586-752b4fbae6e7" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298701 1034 "z=\\sqrt{2}r" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298701 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298701 1111 #uuid "bdd1a213-ca44-5f8e-a993-c39bb868c2b7" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298702 1034 "{z}^{2}={r}^{2}" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298702 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298702 1111 #uuid "8b894cf3-2c3c-5bd9-940e-2a2c403d913c" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298703 1034 "{z}^{2}=2{r}^{2}" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298703 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298703 1111 #uuid "5909e768-6da1-54d5-aa8f-fae130ce052d" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298704 1034 "{z}^{2}=\\sqrt{2}{r}^{2}" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298704 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298704 1111 #uuid "2fe9d632-9f48-5ffd-8392-5752b5ab1536" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298705 1034 "z=2r" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298705 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534610 true] [17592186298705 1111 #uuid "004b9419-b6b0-5a28-b27a-cd0883308785" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298706 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298707 13194139534610 true] [17592186298706 1089 "q-104335" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298706 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534610 true] [17592186298706 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=3r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298706 1113 #uuid "c5c71394-0fe1-554d-95f3-37229cba692d" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298706 1145 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=3r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $3$ times the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{3r}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038052/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" 13194139534610 true] [17592186298707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298708 13194139534610 true] [17592186298707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298709 13194139534